# Backend conventions

## Queues are bounded and drop-oldest

Never `await queue.put(...)` directly from a producer — always go through
`Session.offer(queue, item)`, which evicts the oldest item on overflow.
In a realtime translator, freshness beats completeness.

```python
# BAD - blocks the producer when downstream is slow
await self.session.translation_queue.put(item)

# GOOD - drop-oldest, never blocks
await self.session.offer(self.session.translation_queue, item)
```

## Every queued item carries a `gen` tag

`Session.current_gen` is bumped on every interrupt. Workers must skip stale
items at the top of their loop instead of processing them.

```python
item = await self.session.asr_queue.get()
if item.gen != self.session.current_gen:
    continue  # stale, user has already started a new utterance
```

When emitting a downstream item, copy `gen` from the input item — do not
read `current_gen` again (it may have changed mid-await).

## Cancellable external calls

Any `await` on Whisper, GPT, or Edge TTS must race against
`session.interrupt_event` (see `workers/asr.py::_race_interrupt`) so the user
talking again immediately cancels the in-flight network call.

## No blocking calls

- Use `AsyncOpenAI`, not `OpenAI`.
- Never `time.sleep` — use `asyncio.sleep`.
- VAD frame processing is CPU-light and runs inline in the WS receive loop;
  do not move it to a thread.

## Logging

```python
logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)  # module-level, never print()
```

Log at `INFO` for lifecycle events (start/stop, interrupt, latency) and
`DEBUG` for per-item drops/evictions.
